<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Swedish Pomerania</h1><p class="st-meta">1630 CE – 1815 CE · capital Stettin; later Stralsund</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Baltic territory held by the Swedish crown for nearly two centuries, giving Sweden a foothold and a vote in the Holy Roman Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> landed in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1630 and occupied the duchy during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War. When the last Pomeranian duke died in 1637, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> kept control, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 granted it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Western Pomerania</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> and the island of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Rügen</a>, held as an imperial fief.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Swedish king sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> as Duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Pomerania</a>, but the territory kept its own estates and German law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-greifswald">Greifswald University</a> continued as a centre of learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> and the lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peene">Peene</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1720, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> became the seat of government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> was abolished in 1806 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iv-adolf">Gustav IV Adolf</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a> in 1814 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> ceded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Pomerania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, which exchanged it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815 for Saxe-Lauenburg and money. It became part of the Prussian Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>; Swedish influence survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund's</a> architecture.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/swedish-pomerania.jpg" alt="Swedish Pomerania" width="320" height="174"><figcaption>Image: Wheatley2 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Swedish_Empire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1630 CE – 1815 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stettin; later Stralsund</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christina, Charles XII, Gustav IV Adolf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Low German, Swedish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany, Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Svenska Pommern, Schwedisch-Pommern</dd></dl></aside></div>
